# The `app!` macro

Use `app!` in an application binary when Upwell should own the process host. The macro declares a named item, not an `AppBuilder` expression: it generates the entry point, lifecycle orchestration, CLI integration, and the tooling entry used by `cargo-upwell`.

## Declare a host

Start with the facade prelude and the protocol your binary serves.

<Example title="A named HTTP application">

```rust
use upwell::axum::Axum;
use upwell::prelude::*;
```

Declare one named host with an application name and exactly one protocol:

```rust
app! {
    pub app CatalogApplication {
        name: "catalog-api",
        protocol: Axum,
    }
}
```

</Example>

`CatalogApplication` is the generated host type. Its `name` establishes the application identity and default platform directories; `protocol` selects the transport implementation. Add ordinary plugins for reusable behavior rather than a second protocol.

## Add application setup

Keep application-specific registration and lifecycle work beside the host. The macro passes the configured builder to your callbacks and runs the host lifecycle around serving.

<Example title="Configure before building">

```rust
use upwell::axum::Axum;
use upwell::prelude::*;

fn configure(builder: AppBuilder<Axum>) -> AppBuilder<Axum> {
    builder
        .auto_discover()
        .plugin(MetricsPlugin::default())
}
```

Attach that setup when declaring the host:

```rust
app! {
    pub app CatalogApplication {
        name: "catalog-api",
        protocol: Axum,
        configure: configure,
    }
}
```

</Example>

Use lifecycle callbacks when work belongs to process startup, the completed application, or serving. Use protocol-specific builder methods inside configuration for routes, middleware, and transport settings.

The [canonical `app!` reference](/docs/latest/symbols/upwell_macros/app) lists every macro key, callback shape, and protocol-specific option. It is the source for `services`, components, configuration bindings, managers, CLI commands, and macro path overrides.

## When to use the builder

Choose direct `App::<Protocol>::builder` assembly for tests, embedding, or a process that owns its own entry point. Unlike `app!`, it returns an application value and is followed by `.build().await`; it does not generate a named host, CLI wiring, lifecycle callbacks, or the Cargo Upwell tooling probe.

```rust
let app = App::<Axum>::builder("catalog-api")
    .auto_discover()
    .plugin(MetricsPlugin::default())
    .build()
    .await?;
```
